1
00:00:03,679 --> 00:00:05,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Shall we start?

2
00:00:08,122 --> 00:00:12,765
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how does one lose recollectedness?

3
00:00:16,128 --> 00:00:20,571
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's start by reminding ourselves what recollectedness is.

4
00:00:24,473 --> 00:00:30,197
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how would you... how would you recollect yourself right now?

5
00:00:30,477 --> 00:00:31,158
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you do that?

6
00:00:39,568 --> 00:00:40,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Recollect.

7
00:00:40,969 --> 00:00:41,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember.

8
00:00:43,031 --> 00:00:43,271
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

9
00:00:43,291 --> 00:00:52,959
[SPEAKER_01]: The basis for this experience, which might be, you know, I think, generally, I would go to...

10
00:00:53,159 --> 00:00:55,681
[SPEAKER_00]: So, practically, if I tell you now, recollect yourself, what would you do?

11
00:00:55,721 --> 00:00:56,001
[SPEAKER_00]: Body.

12
00:00:56,401 --> 00:00:56,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Body.

13
00:00:56,881 --> 00:00:57,362
[SPEAKER_01]: For example.

14
00:00:57,602 --> 00:01:00,284
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would recollect something that's already there.

15
00:01:00,384 --> 00:01:00,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

16
00:01:01,525 --> 00:01:01,985
[SPEAKER_01]: On its own.

17
00:01:02,366 --> 00:01:02,806
[SPEAKER_01]: That's right.

18
00:01:03,217 --> 00:01:05,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why the Buddha referred to it as a reference point.

19
00:01:05,679 --> 00:01:07,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

20
00:01:07,161 --> 00:01:10,124
[SPEAKER_00]: The satipaṭṭhāna, the references for your mindfulness.

21
00:01:11,445 --> 00:01:16,370
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in fact, recollectiveness is more like just understanding something.

22
00:01:16,410 --> 00:01:17,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognizing the reference point.

23
00:01:17,771 --> 00:01:18,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, understanding.

24
00:01:18,472 --> 00:01:21,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognizing that which can serve as a reference point, which is your body.

25
00:01:21,535 --> 00:01:21,635
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

26
00:01:21,916 --> 00:01:26,320
[SPEAKER_00]: So, practically speaking, oh, you want to be mindful, so you remember that your body is there.

27
00:01:26,761 --> 00:01:32,643
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you can descend it further and see, oh, this body is a necessary basis for any other experience I can have.

28
00:01:32,743 --> 00:01:37,705
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, you don't need to be looking at the specific experiences or even looking at the body.

29
00:01:37,725 --> 00:01:45,348
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to remember that the body needs to be there as a basis for whatever you're in currently attending to and engage with.

30
00:01:45,949 --> 00:01:50,090
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only reason that you would forget that is because you haven't understood.

31
00:01:50,110 --> 00:01:51,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, so that's that's my next question now.

32
00:01:51,851 --> 00:01:52,071
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

33
00:01:53,267 --> 00:02:03,292
[SPEAKER_00]: If recollectedness, the reference point of your recollection, is not created by you, cannot be created by you, because you're not... my body is real only when I attend it to.

34
00:02:03,592 --> 00:02:03,732
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

35
00:02:04,253 --> 00:02:08,074
[SPEAKER_00]: The body is there which is why you can attend it to, which is why you don't have to attend it to.

36
00:02:08,635 --> 00:02:12,757
[SPEAKER_00]: You can just know it, to the extent necessary for the final knowledge, as the Buddha would say.

37
00:02:13,517 --> 00:02:19,280
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the reference point is not in you, how can you lose your mindfulness then?

38
00:02:20,913 --> 00:02:27,659
[SPEAKER_01]: By thinking that the reference point is in you, by misunderstanding, by not understanding the nature of... Of the body.

39
00:02:27,779 --> 00:02:28,099
[SPEAKER_00]: The body.

40
00:02:28,159 --> 00:02:28,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Of the reference point.

41
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:29,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

42
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:30,721
[SPEAKER_00]: By being doubtful about it.

43
00:02:30,761 --> 00:02:30,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

44
00:02:31,442 --> 00:02:33,544
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and how would that doubt set in?

45
00:02:34,004 --> 00:02:40,430
[SPEAKER_00]: So, say now you're mindful, you're recollected of the body, and then you forget about it.

46
00:02:41,290 --> 00:02:42,912
[SPEAKER_00]: But does that change where the body is?

47
00:02:42,992 --> 00:02:43,192
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

48
00:02:43,573 --> 00:02:44,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that change the nature of the body?

49
00:02:44,854 --> 00:02:45,294
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's not.

50
00:02:45,574 --> 00:02:51,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that change anything that can arise on the body as its basis?

51
00:02:51,218 --> 00:02:51,378
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

52
00:02:51,598 --> 00:02:52,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

53
00:02:54,520 --> 00:03:05,948
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the only thing that could change by you forgetting the correct reference point?

54
00:03:07,189 --> 00:03:09,290
[SPEAKER_00]: By you forgetting... Let's try this.

55
00:03:09,531 --> 00:03:12,893
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not forgetting the reference point, say you calculate the correctness correctly,

56
00:03:13,650 --> 00:03:16,452
[SPEAKER_00]: You're becoming more familiar with the nature of the body.

57
00:03:16,632 --> 00:03:16,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

58
00:03:16,892 --> 00:03:17,893
[SPEAKER_00]: With its characteristics.

59
00:03:17,913 --> 00:03:19,914
[SPEAKER_00]: You're allowing it to endure as it is.

60
00:03:20,275 --> 00:03:22,156
[SPEAKER_00]: As it is, which means you get to see it for what it is.

61
00:03:22,316 --> 00:03:22,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

62
00:03:22,937 --> 00:03:24,718
[SPEAKER_00]: To the extent necessary for the final knowledge.

63
00:03:25,799 --> 00:03:32,243
[SPEAKER_00]: So, forgetting about it is then within the reference point of your mindfulness.

64
00:03:32,263 --> 00:03:32,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

65
00:03:34,585 --> 00:03:37,407
[SPEAKER_00]: So, forgetting about things, it's not how you lose mindfulness.

66
00:03:38,116 --> 00:03:38,717
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be.

67
00:03:38,777 --> 00:03:39,057
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

68
00:03:39,197 --> 00:03:40,799
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about putting things in the wrong order.

69
00:03:40,839 --> 00:03:41,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

70
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:56,778
[SPEAKER_00]: See, when people think that when I forget about my body, that's how I stop being mindful of the body, they still think that the mindfulness is rooted in them attending to things, in them being the center of experience, not in finding the right reference point out there already enduring.

71
00:03:58,197 --> 00:04:05,400
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for as long as you hold on to the view that you are the center of your experience, which is already a form of conceit, you don't understand what mindfulness is.

72
00:04:06,260 --> 00:04:21,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding that mindfulness is independent of you, because of these reference points of the body, feeling, thoughts, and the mind, mind and the thoughts, means sense of self, like self-centered point of view, is within that.

73
00:04:21,746 --> 00:04:25,147
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not first, it's not that which determines it, it's that which is determined by it.

74
00:04:26,288 --> 00:04:30,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Which then means you can forget all you want, your mindfulness remains undiminished.

75
00:04:30,871 --> 00:04:31,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

76
00:04:31,271 --> 00:04:33,112
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when you know your mindfulness is perfected.

77
00:04:33,713 --> 00:04:37,435
[SPEAKER_00]: When forgetting about it, does not induce non-recollectedness.

78
00:04:37,815 --> 00:04:38,696
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it cannot.

79
00:04:39,356 --> 00:04:45,220
[SPEAKER_00]: But, until you know that, that's basically the reason for you losing recollectedness.

80
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:45,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

81
00:04:46,321 --> 00:04:49,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Not having clarity regarding the nature of mindfulness.

82
00:04:49,963 --> 00:04:50,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Not understanding it.

83
00:04:51,504 --> 00:04:55,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Because doesn't matter how much you forget, you cannot lose knowledge through forgetting.

84
00:04:57,475 --> 00:05:08,899
[SPEAKER_00]: That which has been understood becomes impenetrable, becomes indifferent to whether you think about it or not, whether you get distracted by something else or not.

85
00:05:08,919 --> 00:05:15,162
[SPEAKER_00]: The things you understood, you don't need to keep telling to yourself and repeating in order to keep that understanding, because the understanding has taken place.

86
00:05:16,516 --> 00:05:22,257
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we've used the example before, if you know how to drive, you don't have to think about driving.

87
00:05:22,317 --> 00:05:24,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Every individual component of driving.

88
00:05:24,298 --> 00:05:29,499
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you don't attend to that skill, that knowledge, then it's not as clear.

89
00:05:31,219 --> 00:05:34,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, that's what it's meant to practice to the... You're not such a good driver anymore.

90
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:39,041
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it's meant to practice to the extent necessary for the final knowledge.

91
00:05:39,101 --> 00:05:41,361
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not practicing mindfulness for its own sake.

92
00:05:41,881 --> 00:05:44,162
[SPEAKER_00]: You're practicing mindfulness for the sake of dispassion.

93
00:05:45,165 --> 00:05:58,075
[SPEAKER_00]: This disenchantment, letting go of the world, which means the point will come, when you have practiced mindfulness to a sufficient degree, that there is no more basis for any passion to be maintained in your experience, and that's when you're an arahant.

94
00:05:59,256 --> 00:06:06,762
[SPEAKER_00]: And yes, you can stop practicing mindfulness then, but that's kind of impossible, because by that time you already... it wasn't your practice anymore, it was simply your knowledge.

95
00:06:07,302 --> 00:06:13,367
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're saying, so basically, the knowledge that you're talking about, the knowledge of mindfulness, or the knowledge of the body, or whatever,

96
00:06:14,780 --> 00:06:25,444
[SPEAKER_01]: It's beyond a threshold whereby, if you forget about it... Yeah, it's completely... That's not an issue, that's not a problem.

97
00:06:25,464 --> 00:06:27,205
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impenetrable by your forgetfulness.

98
00:06:27,265 --> 00:06:27,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

99
00:06:28,425 --> 00:06:31,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you understand that that forgetting is still on the basis of the body.

100
00:06:31,366 --> 00:06:38,389
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, furthermore, you get to understand that your lapsing in your mindfulness did not start when you forgot about it.

101
00:06:38,529 --> 00:06:38,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

102
00:06:38,849 --> 00:06:41,310
[SPEAKER_00]: It started when you remembered your body again,

103
00:06:42,158 --> 00:06:43,280
[SPEAKER_00]: on a completely different terms.

104
00:06:43,520 --> 00:06:46,804
[SPEAKER_00]: You remember with confusion, remember with mother, remember with doubt.

105
00:06:47,465 --> 00:06:48,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, is this the same?

106
00:06:48,427 --> 00:06:49,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it different?

107
00:06:49,048 --> 00:06:49,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it same or different?

108
00:06:49,989 --> 00:06:50,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it neither?

109
00:06:51,110 --> 00:06:52,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this the same feeling?

110
00:06:52,432 --> 00:06:53,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, let me start again.

111
00:06:53,734 --> 00:06:56,498
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't start again, because you haven't started in the first place.

112
00:06:56,598 --> 00:06:58,200
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only find things being there.

113
00:06:58,220 --> 00:06:59,302
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can only continue.

114
00:07:00,289 --> 00:07:12,455
[SPEAKER_00]: But, see, you prioritizing your idea of what mindfulness is and that my reference, my attending to things, that's where things originate, that's how it comes to be first, means you would actually ignore the fact the thing is already there enduring.

115
00:07:12,856 --> 00:07:16,938
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you wouldn't be discerning it on its own terms, you'd be molding it into what you think it should be.

116
00:07:17,778 --> 00:07:19,779
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when you lose your recollectedness.

117
00:07:20,099 --> 00:07:24,462
[SPEAKER_00]: When you get back to the same thing with confusion, with lack of understanding and lack of clarity.

118
00:07:25,562 --> 00:07:26,723
[SPEAKER_00]: Not when you forget about it.

119
00:07:27,585 --> 00:07:28,106
[SPEAKER_02]: Clarity.

120
00:07:28,326 --> 00:07:28,526
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

121
00:07:28,926 --> 00:07:31,528
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how to maintain clarity?

122
00:07:31,588 --> 00:07:34,610
[SPEAKER_00]: You maintain clarity by understanding the reference point.

123
00:07:35,490 --> 00:07:42,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that clarity is... it has nothing to do with whether you think about it or not, whether you're distracted by something else or not.

124
00:07:43,275 --> 00:07:47,618
[SPEAKER_00]: The clarity of the understood phenomenon, understood nature of mindfulness of the body, remains the same.

125
00:07:49,159 --> 00:07:54,601
[SPEAKER_00]: And the more you develop it, means, the harder for you will be to be confused about it.

126
00:07:54,621 --> 00:07:57,942
[SPEAKER_02]: The clarity can be developed, so that...

127
00:07:58,122 --> 00:07:58,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

128
00:07:59,343 --> 00:08:02,324
[SPEAKER_02]: It's steady, it's clear all the time.

129
00:08:02,624 --> 00:08:03,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

130
00:08:04,225 --> 00:08:06,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what do you mean by all the time?

131
00:08:07,266 --> 00:08:09,127
[SPEAKER_02]: As long as you are attending to it.

132
00:08:09,207 --> 00:08:10,427
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's not a clarity.

133
00:08:10,587 --> 00:08:13,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarity of understanding, not clarity of you attending to it.

134
00:08:13,929 --> 00:08:14,829
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the opposite.

135
00:08:15,309 --> 00:08:15,969
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the opposite.

136
00:08:16,049 --> 00:08:17,490
[SPEAKER_00]: When you are attending to something,

137
00:08:18,086 --> 00:08:19,147
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not your mindfulness.

138
00:08:20,407 --> 00:08:24,129
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness is the reference point that enables you to attend to it.

139
00:08:24,389 --> 00:08:26,571
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you care about the context.

140
00:08:26,991 --> 00:08:27,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

141
00:08:27,671 --> 00:08:31,173
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the clarity of the context is what the clarity of mindfulness is.

142
00:08:31,333 --> 00:08:40,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the clarity of your moment-to-moment attention, which is the result of the whole context that you now sacrifice on account of prioritizing what you are staring at.

143
00:08:41,778 --> 00:08:52,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Can we also say that losing mindfulness, you lose recollecting this mindfulness, whatever, you lose it by not seeing the sameness?

144
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:54,281
[SPEAKER_00]: I was going to come to that now.

145
00:08:54,941 --> 00:08:58,241
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's take another, for example, reference point of the feelings.

146
00:09:00,042 --> 00:09:02,762
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is your current feeling now, overall, fundamentally?

147
00:09:03,022 --> 00:09:03,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasant.

148
00:09:03,482 --> 00:09:05,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasant, agreeable.

149
00:09:05,383 --> 00:09:06,543
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now we carry on talking.

150
00:09:07,313 --> 00:09:16,360
[SPEAKER_00]: And you forget about... Now, see, when you recollect the feeling, you kind of have a thought of what you're currently feeling, but you don't need to attend to it in order to keep feeling it.

151
00:09:18,262 --> 00:09:19,383
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the other way around.

152
00:09:19,663 --> 00:09:23,666
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason you can attend to it is because it's felt already.

153
00:09:24,707 --> 00:09:26,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you can turn your attention towards it.

154
00:09:27,049 --> 00:09:35,596
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't want to be dependent on your attention, equally mindfulness, because it's actually secondary to the presence of that thing that you already kind of know.

155
00:09:36,217 --> 00:09:39,960
[SPEAKER_00]: You want that knowledge that you already have to start equaling mindfulness.

156
00:09:41,060 --> 00:09:48,546
[SPEAKER_00]: So, say you get distracted, you start thinking about something else, and now you want to re-establish your recollectiveness, and you ask yourself, what am I feeling now?

157
00:09:48,706 --> 00:09:49,787
[SPEAKER_00]: And you say, feeling pleasant.

158
00:09:51,268 --> 00:10:04,317
[SPEAKER_00]: What is your attitude towards that reference point that you rediscovered, will determine whether your mindfulness lapsed, or whether it's still the same enduring, that has now included the attitude of forgetting this particular thing.

159
00:10:11,162 --> 00:10:19,486
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you get back to the feeling now, after having been thinking about it, do you think it's a different feeling?

160
00:10:19,506 --> 00:10:19,666
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

161
00:10:20,827 --> 00:10:21,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it the same feeling?

162
00:10:22,888 --> 00:10:24,188
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't think so.

163
00:10:24,649 --> 00:10:27,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you have any idea where it was while you were not thinking about it?

164
00:10:30,151 --> 00:10:30,332
No.

165
00:10:31,572 --> 00:10:32,072
[SPEAKER_02]: Where it was?

166
00:10:32,293 --> 00:10:34,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, where was it while you were not thinking about it?

167
00:10:34,374 --> 00:10:36,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Did it disappear and then come back, the same thing?

168
00:10:37,135 --> 00:10:37,475
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

169
00:10:37,495 --> 00:10:38,736
[SPEAKER_00]: So, was it still there enduring?

170
00:10:38,976 --> 00:10:39,176
Yes.

171
00:10:39,788 --> 00:10:49,714
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the feeling, the reference point of mindfulness can endure then, unchanged, in its identity, so to speak, without needing you to attend to it constantly.

172
00:10:49,914 --> 00:10:53,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I'm feeling this, I'm feeling this, I'm feeling, feeling, touching, touching, moving, moving.

173
00:10:53,817 --> 00:10:55,798
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to keep repeating that, yet it's there.

174
00:10:56,358 --> 00:10:57,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's only the beginning of the knowledge.

175
00:10:59,480 --> 00:11:05,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Next time, you'll be less wound up when you forget about things, and you will not try to restart your entire practice,

176
00:11:06,073 --> 00:11:09,354
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll just... no, I just need to recollect it correctly, because it was always there.

177
00:11:10,454 --> 00:11:12,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, if you find out a different feeling.

178
00:11:13,334 --> 00:11:15,595
[SPEAKER_00]: It's only different in its content.

179
00:11:15,615 --> 00:11:18,095
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature of feeling remains the same.

180
00:11:18,495 --> 00:11:18,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Enduring.

181
00:11:19,656 --> 00:11:20,416
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the whole point.

182
00:11:20,996 --> 00:11:26,757
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's how you recollect it, determines whether there is the right context being established or not.

183
00:11:26,857 --> 00:11:28,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Not whether you forget about it or not.

184
00:11:31,678 --> 00:11:32,718
[SPEAKER_02]: The knowledge remains.

185
00:11:33,799 --> 00:11:33,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

186
00:11:34,219 --> 00:11:37,601
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's that knowledge that you get to develop layer by layer by layer.

187
00:11:37,761 --> 00:11:41,664
[SPEAKER_01]: But the knowledge remains the same thing because...

188
00:11:41,864 --> 00:11:43,405
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not dependent on your attention.

189
00:11:43,425 --> 00:11:49,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that thing depends... that feeling, or let's say the nature of feeling, remains.

190
00:11:49,629 --> 00:11:49,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

191
00:11:50,009 --> 00:11:50,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

192
00:11:50,249 --> 00:11:51,170
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the knowledge is.

193
00:11:51,470 --> 00:11:51,670
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

194
00:11:51,870 --> 00:11:53,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in whether you attend to it or not.

195
00:11:55,432 --> 00:11:56,893
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the knowledge gets developed.

196
00:11:57,473 --> 00:11:58,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Mmm.

197
00:11:58,234 --> 00:11:59,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Consciousness gets understood.

198
00:12:00,915 --> 00:12:01,676
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said.

199
00:12:05,761 --> 00:12:11,546
[SPEAKER_01]: But I think the tendency for most people is to prioritize their point of view.

200
00:12:11,566 --> 00:12:12,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Change.

201
00:12:12,667 --> 00:12:18,852
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so, you might have... you might reckon, like, okay, I feel a pleasant feeling now.

202
00:12:19,453 --> 00:12:21,334
[SPEAKER_01]: I was feeling pleasant the whole morning.

203
00:12:22,835 --> 00:12:28,320
[SPEAKER_01]: But the tendency, I think, is to put first that what's changed.

204
00:12:28,660 --> 00:12:28,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

205
00:12:29,341 --> 00:12:32,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's because it's it's rooted in that view.

206
00:12:33,553 --> 00:12:38,634
[SPEAKER_00]: That you can impartially, externally, observe your experience without being the partaker in it.

207
00:12:38,734 --> 00:12:48,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Or rather, at the same time, while you're partaking in it, you can have this completely independent point of view upon your experience, point of view that's independent of your experience.

208
00:12:48,477 --> 00:12:54,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is the idea of science, which is the idea of... common idea of when people say everything is impermanent, thus I am impermanent.

209
00:12:55,308 --> 00:13:03,915
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why people would then just... even when there is the same feeling enduring, they could not bear, they could not understand the idea of a thing enduring, unchanging.

210
00:13:04,075 --> 00:13:04,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

211
00:13:04,916 --> 00:13:08,098
[SPEAKER_00]: They wouldn't say, well, it can't be the same feeling, because six seconds have passed.

212
00:13:08,118 --> 00:13:08,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

213
00:13:09,039 --> 00:13:11,200
[SPEAKER_00]: And in my view, every second is a different entity.

214
00:13:11,220 --> 00:13:15,284
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if you were to pursue their view, you would realize it contains a contradiction.

215
00:13:16,166 --> 00:13:18,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's a millisecond within a millisecond.

216
00:13:18,467 --> 00:13:25,811
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you even utter a word and designate identity of something, if it's changing at every, like, those tiny microscopic levels?

217
00:13:26,111 --> 00:13:32,234
[SPEAKER_01]: But, there can't be, in something enjoying the same, there can't be sameness without there also being change at a more particular level.

218
00:13:32,254 --> 00:13:32,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

219
00:13:32,474 --> 00:13:36,536
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was going to say that, that the point is not like, oh, I must...

220
00:13:37,817 --> 00:13:42,059
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about denying the fact that things endure for five seconds or ten seconds.

221
00:13:42,139 --> 00:13:44,540
[SPEAKER_00]: It's to recognize that it's

222
00:13:45,736 --> 00:13:51,998
[SPEAKER_00]: Your notion of the thing comes first, which is why you can discern the endurance of it, as in its particular change.

223
00:13:52,738 --> 00:13:57,199
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is no unifying identity of that thing, the change would not be perceivable.

224
00:13:57,379 --> 00:13:59,359
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think that's what the mistake is, that people...

225
00:13:59,399 --> 00:14:00,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Putting first, what's second?

226
00:14:00,300 --> 00:14:00,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

227
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:03,000
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people prioritize the more particular...

228
00:14:03,240 --> 00:14:03,540
[SPEAKER_01]: The change.

229
00:14:03,560 --> 00:14:05,341
[SPEAKER_01]: The more particular aspect which have changed.

230
00:14:05,361 --> 00:14:05,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

231
00:14:06,021 --> 00:14:07,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is also... Because that's in front.

232
00:14:07,622 --> 00:14:09,544
[SPEAKER_01]: That is, like, the kind of... First thing you see.

233
00:14:09,664 --> 00:14:10,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

234
00:14:11,026 --> 00:14:12,827
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will remain on that level.

235
00:14:13,007 --> 00:14:18,532
[SPEAKER_00]: That will always be the first thing you see for as long as you also, uh, habitually act out of sensuality.

236
00:14:18,732 --> 00:14:20,534
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can... That's also the first thing you see.

237
00:14:20,714 --> 00:14:21,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense objects.

238
00:14:21,555 --> 00:14:22,736
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't see sense restraint.

239
00:14:22,936 --> 00:14:34,919
[SPEAKER_01]: But those... so you have to learn how to look at the periphery, like look at the background, because those particular changes can only be particular changes of a thing.

240
00:14:35,019 --> 00:14:39,841
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you just need to find a reference point that remains the same throughout this change.

241
00:14:40,581 --> 00:14:42,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what mindfulness is.

242
00:14:42,482 --> 00:14:48,264
[SPEAKER_00]: And not try to reinterpret the reference point through the change, but try to understand the change through the reference point.

243
00:14:48,704 --> 00:14:53,585
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can say, oh, this morning I felt like this, then I felt like that, then he said something else, then it was this, then I done that.

244
00:14:53,945 --> 00:14:55,926
[SPEAKER_00]: What was unchanged through that?

245
00:14:56,046 --> 00:14:59,707
[SPEAKER_00]: It was, oh, the something that I... the fact that my body was there.

246
00:14:59,747 --> 00:15:01,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of my body was unchanged.

247
00:15:02,548 --> 00:15:06,109
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is unchanged through all the different postures, all the different bodily actions?

248
00:15:06,229 --> 00:15:07,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of the body is unchanged.

249
00:15:08,469 --> 00:15:14,855
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is exactly why you can discern all these, like, exactly, sub-general, more particular changes.

250
00:15:15,376 --> 00:15:26,146
[SPEAKER_00]: But, no, you choose to put that first and say that I have the body because of all of these molecular changes in constant elements that I can only observe because I have the body first.

251
00:15:27,237 --> 00:15:28,258
[SPEAKER_00]: It's reductionism.

252
00:15:28,278 --> 00:15:30,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, everything can be reduced to the most particular.

253
00:15:30,679 --> 00:15:34,280
[SPEAKER_00]: And then people try to understand the nīcā through the same kind of lens.

254
00:15:34,541 --> 00:15:35,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying, yeah, look, everything is constant.

255
00:15:36,001 --> 00:15:38,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Look, things disappear, thus they're impermanent.

256
00:15:38,102 --> 00:15:39,043
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not what impermanence is.

257
00:15:39,063 --> 00:15:39,343
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

258
00:15:39,683 --> 00:15:42,164
[SPEAKER_01]: So, impermanence is only to be discovered.

259
00:15:42,184 --> 00:15:46,306
[SPEAKER_00]: When things are not disappeared, while you're still alive, that's the only way you can understand impermanence.

260
00:15:46,386 --> 00:15:49,828
[SPEAKER_00]: If you wait for death to be proof of impermanence, it's too late.

261
00:15:50,688 --> 00:15:52,029
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not impermanence, by the way.

262
00:15:52,069 --> 00:15:53,029
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just another change.

263
00:15:53,550 --> 00:15:53,830
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

264
00:15:55,469 --> 00:15:58,011
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not freeing yourself from the fact that things will disappear.

265
00:15:58,051 --> 00:16:01,774
[SPEAKER_00]: You're freeing yourself that you're constantly within the change and partaking in the change.

266
00:16:02,395 --> 00:16:06,418
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will for as long as you put that first and don't see the nature of that which is changing.

267
00:16:07,359 --> 00:16:09,601
[SPEAKER_00]: So, endurance is in the completely opposite direction.

268
00:16:09,661 --> 00:16:10,041
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in the...

269
00:16:10,222 --> 00:16:12,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, impermanence is in the completely different direction.

270
00:16:12,924 --> 00:16:17,528
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in the direction of what is actually enduring, what's remaining the same.

271
00:16:19,803 --> 00:16:20,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, exactly.

272
00:16:21,405 --> 00:16:29,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Which actually, now I say, it sounds like a contradiction, it sounds like, but that's exactly where... That's where the sotāpanna is inside, is whatever has the nature of manifestation.

273
00:16:30,115 --> 00:16:31,396
[SPEAKER_00]: That is why it's impermanent.

274
00:16:31,436 --> 00:16:34,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because I also waited long enough to see it disappear.

275
00:16:35,441 --> 00:16:39,726
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's because it's right there and I get to see it and because that nature is present, I get to understand it.

276
00:16:40,647 --> 00:16:40,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

277
00:16:41,498 --> 00:16:44,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Because cessation is manifested, as the Buddha said.

278
00:16:44,300 --> 00:16:45,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Arising is manifested.

279
00:16:45,621 --> 00:16:46,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation is manifested.

280
00:16:47,503 --> 00:16:49,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Impermanence is on the level of manifestation.

281
00:16:51,066 --> 00:16:55,710
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, seeing that, the nature of, for example, body as being

282
00:16:57,144 --> 00:17:00,866
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's basically, it's already enduring whether I attend to it or not.

283
00:17:01,066 --> 00:17:01,906
[SPEAKER_01]: It's got nothing to do with me.

284
00:17:01,926 --> 00:17:02,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether I forget about it or not.

285
00:17:02,967 --> 00:17:03,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether I forget about it or not.

286
00:17:03,787 --> 00:17:04,588
[SPEAKER_01]: It's got nothing to do with me.

287
00:17:05,748 --> 00:17:08,069
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the nature of that thing has nothing to do with me.

288
00:17:08,209 --> 00:17:08,449
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

289
00:17:08,469 --> 00:17:09,550
[SPEAKER_00]: I have no control over it.

290
00:17:09,930 --> 00:17:10,611
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where I'm coming from.

291
00:17:10,651 --> 00:17:17,634
[SPEAKER_00]: And if I keep putting that first, I will gradually undermine my own gratuitous sense of that I am first.

292
00:17:17,714 --> 00:17:17,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

293
00:17:18,234 --> 00:17:20,155
[SPEAKER_00]: I am the master controller of this body.

294
00:17:22,976 --> 00:17:23,917
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the development.

295
00:17:25,508 --> 00:17:26,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

296
00:17:26,629 --> 00:17:29,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Development of the nature thing that will have to result in dispassion.

297
00:17:35,374 --> 00:17:39,957
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I keep saying in previous talks, you know, come back to the present moment.

298
00:17:40,578 --> 00:17:42,459
[SPEAKER_00]: You couldn't have left it even if you wanted it.

299
00:17:43,598 --> 00:17:48,160
[SPEAKER_00]: But the idea that you hold the view that you can return implies that you can leave.

300
00:17:49,061 --> 00:17:50,421
[SPEAKER_00]: So, come back to the body.

301
00:17:50,941 --> 00:17:56,584
[SPEAKER_00]: If you still subscribe to the view that you can come back to it, it carries the implication that you could have left.

302
00:17:57,224 --> 00:18:02,447
[SPEAKER_00]: And for as long as you hold that view, you'll never understand that whether you leave or go, you are dependent on the body right there.

303
00:18:03,147 --> 00:18:06,829
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your practice is to keep returning to the present moment, you are...

304
00:18:07,772 --> 00:18:08,394
[SPEAKER_00]: Intentionally.

305
00:18:08,634 --> 00:18:10,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Maintaining a wrong view that you can leave the present moment.

306
00:18:10,799 --> 00:18:12,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Removing, yeah.

307
00:18:12,443 --> 00:18:13,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Assuming that you can.

308
00:18:13,706 --> 00:18:15,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, ironically, keeps you away from the present moment.

309
00:18:15,671 --> 00:18:15,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

310
00:18:19,443 --> 00:18:23,106
[SPEAKER_00]: But, so, like, when you recollect it, it's not about attending to the body here.

311
00:18:23,227 --> 00:18:34,077
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like understanding the body that is here, understanding the feeling that is here, understanding the mind that is here, understanding the thoughts that are here, as a necessary basis for everything else that I can attend on account of it.

312
00:18:34,097 --> 00:18:38,581
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you said, knowledge is to be developed, consciousness is to be understood.

313
00:18:38,921 --> 00:18:39,101
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

314
00:18:39,562 --> 00:18:40,263
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the same thing.

315
00:18:40,303 --> 00:18:40,483
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

316
00:18:40,803 --> 00:18:41,604
[SPEAKER_02]: Consciousness, that's...

317
00:18:43,777 --> 00:18:44,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Consciousness as the aggregate.

318
00:18:44,998 --> 00:18:59,343
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because usually consciousness is where my point of view is, but then you realize, understanding that there is no such thing as an independent point of view, independent of the existing body, existing feeling, existing mind, existing thoughts, means there's no such thing as consciousness independent of the existing matter and everything else.

319
00:19:00,424 --> 00:19:03,085
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your sense of self, your self-centeredness,

320
00:19:03,932 --> 00:19:08,994
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody denies that it's there, but you see it now a second, which means that's not the owner, that's not the master.

321
00:19:09,034 --> 00:19:09,694
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... it's...

322
00:19:09,714 --> 00:19:12,535
[SPEAKER_00]: When these things disappear, my sense of self cannot remain.

323
00:19:12,675 --> 00:19:14,276
[SPEAKER_00]: That's basically experience of not-self.

324
00:19:15,176 --> 00:19:19,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Not through denying that which is self, but seeing it is fully dependent on this, and you fully understand it.

325
00:19:22,119 --> 00:19:25,960
[SPEAKER_00]: So, lack of understanding means putting the... your point of view first.

326
00:19:28,261 --> 00:19:31,242
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a lack of understanding.

327
00:19:32,272 --> 00:19:34,532
[SPEAKER_02]: Not understanding consciousness, not understanding aggregates.

328
00:19:35,513 --> 00:19:35,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

329
00:19:35,893 --> 00:19:37,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's not go into aggregates now.

330
00:19:37,213 --> 00:19:38,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's stick with the mindfulness.

331
00:19:39,293 --> 00:19:41,154
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yes, it corresponds to the same thing.

332
00:19:48,275 --> 00:19:54,396
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mindfulness is always that move, or like a move, that understanding.

333
00:19:54,436 --> 00:19:55,636
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the understanding, isn't it?

334
00:19:55,896 --> 00:19:58,697
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, yeah, recollecting the conflicts that's been clarified.

335
00:19:58,737 --> 00:20:00,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Putting the right thing first.

336
00:20:00,477 --> 00:20:00,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

337
00:20:01,669 --> 00:20:06,613
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you need to have knowledge, you know, in order for mindfulness to be right.

338
00:20:08,775 --> 00:20:10,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Samādhi always comes first.

339
00:20:11,116 --> 00:20:14,059
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you don't know what the context is, how can you recollect it?

340
00:20:14,279 --> 00:20:15,140
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you discern it?

341
00:20:17,061 --> 00:20:21,885
[SPEAKER_00]: If the idea of mindfulness for you is attending to my body here and now, means exactly that.

342
00:20:21,985 --> 00:20:25,468
[SPEAKER_00]: When I don't attend to it, my mindfulness is gone, because that's the only mindfulness that I know.

343
00:20:27,336 --> 00:20:32,498
[SPEAKER_00]: But if the mindfulness is based on discernment and the knowledge, that can't go, because it's independent of what you choose to attend to.

344
00:20:33,318 --> 00:20:38,800
[SPEAKER_00]: All you need to do is protect the context, by not contradicting the context, through acting out of greed, aversion, delusion.

345
00:20:40,960 --> 00:20:43,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you said this before, you...

346
00:20:44,761 --> 00:20:46,082
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you realize if...

347
00:20:47,805 --> 00:20:49,286
[SPEAKER_01]: You've forgotten the context.

348
00:20:50,546 --> 00:20:55,108
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize that that forgetting is included within that context.

349
00:20:55,128 --> 00:20:56,689
[SPEAKER_01]: It's secondary within the enduring body.

350
00:20:57,009 --> 00:21:01,591
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't possibly have been here having forgotten about my body if there wasn't a body here.

351
00:21:01,891 --> 00:21:02,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, impossible.

352
00:21:03,292 --> 00:21:07,514
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't matter the extent of your forgetfulness, the extent of your remembering it.

353
00:21:07,654 --> 00:21:11,796
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have understood the reference point correctly, it will be included properly.

354
00:21:12,276 --> 00:21:14,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you cannot be forgetful.

355
00:21:16,735 --> 00:21:18,297
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is going beyond the threshold.

356
00:21:18,577 --> 00:21:22,681
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... that's a kind of... Yeah, that's already stepping across the threshold of that knowledge.

357
00:21:22,761 --> 00:21:23,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

358
00:21:23,082 --> 00:21:24,964
[SPEAKER_00]: And now you're partaking that right mindfulness.

359
00:21:25,064 --> 00:21:25,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

360
00:21:26,165 --> 00:21:36,496
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, even if you... if you got completely distracted with, I don't know, to do something that required, like, this full-on concentration, when you stop doing it,

361
00:21:37,455 --> 00:21:40,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Your context is back, because it could have never left, and you understood that.

362
00:21:40,817 --> 00:21:45,599
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the context re... re-emerges, so to speak, you don't mistake it, you're not confused.

363
00:21:45,999 --> 00:21:46,939
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't, eh...

364
00:21:47,920 --> 00:21:55,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Your confusion in regard to the re-emerging context of your body being there on a necessary basis, does not induce you to think you are first.

365
00:21:55,763 --> 00:21:56,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

366
00:21:57,464 --> 00:21:59,645
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you have gone beyond the doubt as well.

367
00:22:01,018 --> 00:22:03,639
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when Buddha asked monks, monks, do you see this has arisen?

368
00:22:03,819 --> 00:22:04,399
[SPEAKER_00]: This is present.

369
00:22:04,479 --> 00:22:05,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see this is manifested?

370
00:22:05,799 --> 00:22:06,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, we do, sir.

371
00:22:07,319 --> 00:22:13,441
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see that on account of that being manifested as a necessary basis, this comes to be as well?

372
00:22:14,101 --> 00:22:14,841
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, yes, we do.

373
00:22:15,241 --> 00:22:20,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Monks, the one who is not clear that this comes first and this then comes second, is there doubt arising in their mind?

374
00:22:20,882 --> 00:22:21,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it does.

375
00:22:22,063 --> 00:22:22,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they are not clear.

376
00:22:24,163 --> 00:22:28,224
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just doubt means confusion in regard to the right order.

377
00:22:29,442 --> 00:22:32,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Lapse of mindfulness means confusion in regard to the right order.

378
00:22:32,324 --> 00:22:48,292
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it means that you could still... it's possible for you to still... as you... when you remember, suddenly you realize you've forgotten presence and body, there still remains a possibility for you to think that that loss was because you weren't paying attention to it.

379
00:22:48,312 --> 00:22:50,133
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it was you... your fault.

380
00:22:50,273 --> 00:22:50,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

381
00:22:50,833 --> 00:22:51,594
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all...

382
00:22:51,634 --> 00:22:52,915
[SPEAKER_00]: I haven't attended to it hard enough.

383
00:22:53,155 --> 00:22:53,395
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm...

384
00:22:54,015 --> 00:22:54,575
[SPEAKER_00]: I come first.

385
00:22:55,456 --> 00:22:58,818
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's... it's really... it's about learning how to forget mindfully.

386
00:22:59,743 --> 00:23:01,604
[SPEAKER_00]: And not about preventing forgetfulness.

387
00:23:01,704 --> 00:23:02,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

388
00:23:02,824 --> 00:23:06,525
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's like even Vajrajan Chah describing one of his talks when he was practicing meditation.

389
00:23:07,406 --> 00:23:11,367
[SPEAKER_00]: He was all wound up trying to concentrate on his breath every second, every second.

390
00:23:12,127 --> 00:23:13,788
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he just... you can't sustain that.

391
00:23:13,888 --> 00:23:16,269
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he said, no, what if I let my mind think?

392
00:23:16,289 --> 00:23:23,491
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he would let his mind think and he would go further and go further and keep a corner of your eye at it, at the mind.

393
00:23:23,791 --> 00:23:26,372
[SPEAKER_00]: But inevitably, it will have to pass the point when you forget.

394
00:23:27,581 --> 00:23:36,665
[SPEAKER_00]: And he would allow that to happen as well, and only stop it when that forgetfulness would then carry on and start thinking about lustful thoughts or grief.

395
00:23:37,285 --> 00:23:37,985
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's obvious.

396
00:23:38,025 --> 00:23:41,847
[SPEAKER_00]: That's something you cannot not be aware of, because it starts involving your choice.

397
00:23:42,067 --> 00:23:42,727
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when you...

398
00:23:42,747 --> 00:23:44,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, now I'll restart.

399
00:23:44,688 --> 00:23:45,909
[SPEAKER_00]: But not before that point.

400
00:23:46,409 --> 00:23:48,650
[SPEAKER_00]: But usually people now restart every second.

401
00:23:49,470 --> 00:23:51,831
[SPEAKER_00]: You restart for the sake of next restarting.

402
00:23:52,950 --> 00:23:58,115
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what I mean, like, when I kind of semi-jokingly say, it's like throwing an anchor, but you don't wait to be anchored.

403
00:23:58,555 --> 00:24:00,037
[SPEAKER_00]: You pull it and then throw it.

404
00:24:00,057 --> 00:24:04,341
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your purpose of anchoring is just to keep chucking the anchor into the water and not let it anchor you.

405
00:24:06,523 --> 00:24:15,952
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to learn how to forget mindfully and then you will not be able to lose the context, nor will you be able to kind of drift into the sensuality, ill-will and distraction.

406
00:24:18,503 --> 00:24:25,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that requires choices that you make on account of the doubt, on account of the confusion regarding the context.

407
00:24:25,973 --> 00:24:27,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Not on account of forgetfulness.

408
00:24:27,895 --> 00:24:32,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because if you can't accidentally, sorry, you can't accidentally just unaware...

409
00:24:33,645 --> 00:24:37,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Get fully engaged with sensuality or start harming somebody physically.

410
00:24:37,368 --> 00:24:42,691
[SPEAKER_00]: It needs to be on the level of choice that you would make if the basis is muddled.

411
00:24:43,332 --> 00:24:44,713
[SPEAKER_01]: But you would... that's what I was going to say.

412
00:24:44,733 --> 00:24:50,837
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't... you wouldn't make those choices if you realize that, no, that necessary basis comes first.

413
00:24:50,897 --> 00:24:51,697
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you wouldn't.

414
00:24:52,177 --> 00:24:53,739
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be impossible for you to make those choices.

415
00:24:53,819 --> 00:24:58,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Any unwholesome choice is always based on you trying to control

416
00:24:59,022 --> 00:24:59,462
[SPEAKER_00]: The feeling.

417
00:24:59,622 --> 00:25:00,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

418
00:25:00,263 --> 00:25:00,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

419
00:25:00,663 --> 00:25:01,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Putting yourself first.

420
00:25:01,284 --> 00:25:02,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Putting yourself first.

421
00:25:02,104 --> 00:25:04,846
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm the master, thus I have every right to try to control this.

422
00:25:04,886 --> 00:25:05,046
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

423
00:25:06,127 --> 00:25:08,048
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why mindfulness, you can't have too much of it.

424
00:25:08,168 --> 00:25:08,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

425
00:25:08,749 --> 00:25:10,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not doing it.

426
00:25:10,570 --> 00:25:13,692
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just recognition, but it's already there, and there can't be too much of it.

427
00:25:13,872 --> 00:25:15,173
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why restraint comes first.

428
00:25:15,373 --> 00:25:19,396
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why restraint comes first, and that's why, for Arahant, mindfulness is completely effortless.

429
00:25:19,556 --> 00:25:24,179
[SPEAKER_00]: And he doesn't need to do it, but he cannot not do it, because it's not about doing it anymore.

430
00:25:24,199 --> 00:25:25,400
[SPEAKER_00]: His discernment is on that level.

431
00:25:26,481 --> 00:25:26,561
[SPEAKER_00]: So

432
00:25:28,783 --> 00:25:30,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it different?

433
00:25:31,126 --> 00:25:36,772
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's been like 15 minutes, and the same thing has been enduring in the background, even without you needing to think about it.

434
00:25:40,519 --> 00:25:57,290
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize... See, even if you don't explicitly think, oh, that feeling comes first, your point of view comes second as a result of it, your attention comes second, you already start to discern that it comes first because it was there independent of all of your movements, actions, thoughts, words you said, things you attended to.

435
00:25:57,330 --> 00:26:00,192
[SPEAKER_02]: And you just... the more you... you become familiar with that.

436
00:26:00,572 --> 00:26:00,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

437
00:26:00,932 --> 00:26:02,753
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that familiarity you want to develop.

438
00:26:03,333 --> 00:26:06,194
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when it goes away, it doesn't come back as a stranger.

439
00:26:06,454 --> 00:26:06,814
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

440
00:26:07,234 --> 00:26:08,315
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the familiarity.

441
00:26:08,395 --> 00:26:09,315
[SPEAKER_00]: That thing that's always been there.

442
00:26:09,595 --> 00:26:10,416
[SPEAKER_00]: That you understood.

443
00:26:10,436 --> 00:26:10,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

444
00:26:10,956 --> 00:26:13,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means the whole setup comes back with it.

445
00:26:13,397 --> 00:26:13,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

446
00:26:14,617 --> 00:26:24,561
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also why all you need to do, once you fully develop sensory strength, all you need to do is practice mindfulness and you won't be even pressured by sensuality anymore, eventually.

447
00:26:24,641 --> 00:26:24,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Because

448
00:26:26,290 --> 00:26:36,876
[SPEAKER_00]: See, when you cannot pervert the order that this feeling or this body comes first, you cannot then give in to what you want to attend as coming first, which is sense object.

449
00:26:37,296 --> 00:26:41,298
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't matter what sense object comes your way, you see it within this context that's actually...

450
00:26:41,318 --> 00:26:43,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if I engage with sensuality, this cannot change.

451
00:26:45,040 --> 00:26:46,281
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, you can't forget it.

452
00:26:46,321 --> 00:26:47,982
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, you can't engage with it.

453
00:26:49,177 --> 00:26:59,223
[SPEAKER_00]: It requires you to turn away from that context, but not just to ignore it, but to ignore it on the pretext of pretending, I am first, thus I will still do what I want to do.

454
00:27:00,864 --> 00:27:08,609
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you also get to eventually understand that it doesn't matter the power and the extent of your volition and your will, this order can never change.

455
00:27:09,109 --> 00:27:12,411
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when you abandon your own choice, the ownership of it.

456
00:27:13,492 --> 00:27:15,733
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through that indirect recognition of the right order.

457
00:27:20,845 --> 00:27:31,058
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but you can't make choices which are based on passion, killing someone, sexual intercourse.

458
00:27:31,078 --> 00:27:32,720
[SPEAKER_00]: And expect to maintain the right context.

459
00:27:32,800 --> 00:27:32,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

460
00:27:33,080 --> 00:27:34,803
[SPEAKER_00]: No, because that requires the wrong order.

461
00:27:35,083 --> 00:27:36,625
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that right order.

462
00:27:37,030 --> 00:27:38,030
[SPEAKER_02]: Takes away the passion.

463
00:27:38,070 --> 00:27:38,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

464
00:27:38,490 --> 00:27:51,474
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the extent of the action that you just said, the extent of sexual intercourse, the extent of killing somebody, is... requires you being first, in your view, in your point of view, putting that first.

465
00:27:51,654 --> 00:27:53,154
[SPEAKER_00]: It requires it as a basis.

466
00:27:53,174 --> 00:27:58,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Can we say because it's... because basically you're assuming that you have some level of control over your feeling?

467
00:27:58,675 --> 00:27:58,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

468
00:27:59,255 --> 00:28:04,757
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's that assumption of that control that... it's basically the extent of that assumption.

469
00:28:05,337 --> 00:28:05,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

470
00:28:06,505 --> 00:28:14,127
[SPEAKER_00]: It is required, to a certain extent, for you to act physically, either towards sensuality or towards karmic.

471
00:28:14,727 --> 00:28:16,208
[SPEAKER_00]: You may have the assumption without acting.

472
00:28:16,488 --> 00:28:16,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

473
00:28:17,288 --> 00:28:17,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Which needs to be...

474
00:28:17,888 --> 00:28:20,989
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to stop acting, because you stop fueling the assumption.

475
00:28:21,289 --> 00:28:25,670
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you get to understand the assumption, and once it's understood, it's gone, and then you cannot act like that.

476
00:28:31,072 --> 00:28:34,953
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, like, Arahant is incapable of sexual intercourse or intentional killing.

477
00:28:35,353 --> 00:28:35,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because

478
00:28:36,977 --> 00:28:40,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Those acts have lost significance and he doesn't even know what that is anymore.

479
00:28:41,478 --> 00:28:49,560
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's because he cannot, sort of, twist his mind to the extent necessary for those acts to be intentionally done.

480
00:28:49,580 --> 00:28:54,602
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... it's not also because he's got... because of all that willpower that... No, no, no, no.

481
00:28:54,642 --> 00:28:56,262
[SPEAKER_01]: He's just got, you know... Yeah, yeah, yeah.

482
00:28:56,943 --> 00:29:01,024
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he's understanding it's... No, it's just the mind cannot be perverted to that extent anymore.

483
00:29:01,164 --> 00:29:02,004
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just impossible.

484
00:29:06,566 --> 00:29:16,596
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, alternatively, then you can, you know, if the feeling... Again, when I say the feeling, usually it should be discerned on the level of the mood, if you want to practically start developing what we're talking about.

485
00:29:17,036 --> 00:29:17,917
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, you can do your body.

486
00:29:17,937 --> 00:29:19,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is your current body posture?

487
00:29:19,498 --> 00:29:22,161
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever the body posture is present, means the body needs to be there.

488
00:29:22,802 --> 00:29:26,345
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start understanding necessity of the body through the current body posture.

489
00:29:27,186 --> 00:29:31,350
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see, again, we've been speaking for like half an hour or something.

490
00:29:32,280 --> 00:29:35,181
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind was going left and right, explaining different phasses, different things.

491
00:29:35,621 --> 00:29:37,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Your posture has remained unchanged.

492
00:29:38,382 --> 00:29:43,404
[SPEAKER_00]: And you didn't have to attend to it, and you didn't have to keep telling to yourself that you must be seated.

493
00:29:43,424 --> 00:29:49,266
[SPEAKER_00]: And you forgot about your body posture numerous amount of times, yet the reference point of mindfulness is still there.

494
00:29:50,647 --> 00:29:53,308
[SPEAKER_00]: So you realize, ah, so it's really how I remember it.

495
00:29:54,528 --> 00:30:00,750
[SPEAKER_00]: If I remember it with that clarity, with the right context, if I recognize it for what it is, that's how you maintain your mindfulness.

496
00:30:00,810 --> 00:30:04,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Not by having the kind of, you know, the non-lapsing mindfulness.

497
00:30:04,291 --> 00:30:08,593
[SPEAKER_00]: People think the idea is you just hold so tightly every second of your life for the rest of your life.

498
00:30:08,813 --> 00:30:09,473
[SPEAKER_00]: One-pointedness.

499
00:30:09,673 --> 00:30:09,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

500
00:30:10,153 --> 00:30:10,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

501
00:30:10,453 --> 00:30:11,134
[SPEAKER_00]: One-pointedness.

502
00:30:11,194 --> 00:30:11,934
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's completely opposite.

503
00:30:11,954 --> 00:30:14,535
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just you're unable to not recognize it.

504
00:30:14,655 --> 00:30:14,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

505
00:30:15,235 --> 00:30:16,655
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot mistake it anymore.

506
00:30:16,835 --> 00:30:19,116
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when mindfulness is developed to the extent necessary.

507
00:30:19,616 --> 00:30:23,218
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I could focus on the fact that my posture is maybe slightly changed.

508
00:30:23,238 --> 00:30:23,598
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like

509
00:30:25,781 --> 00:30:27,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Your arms have changed, your legs have changed.

510
00:30:28,002 --> 00:30:31,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Within the same identity of the same seated position.

511
00:30:31,784 --> 00:30:37,828
[SPEAKER_01]: So, could you say, if somebody... if you notice that you're suddenly in a different mood...

512
00:30:40,523 --> 00:30:41,784
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what's in common to a good mood?

513
00:30:41,824 --> 00:30:42,544
[SPEAKER_00]: What's in common to a bad mood?

514
00:30:42,564 --> 00:30:43,184
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I'm saying.

515
00:30:43,284 --> 00:30:45,806
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, make that move towards...

516
00:30:45,846 --> 00:30:46,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Include...

517
00:30:46,346 --> 00:30:46,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Hang on.

518
00:30:46,946 --> 00:30:47,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, okay.

519
00:30:47,407 --> 00:30:48,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Include it within.

520
00:30:48,507 --> 00:30:52,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Include the mood within the greater identity of the unchanged thing.

521
00:30:52,349 --> 00:30:54,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Which makes it possible... For the mood to change.

522
00:30:54,931 --> 00:30:58,592
[SPEAKER_01]: ...for those different things to be the same, to be a change.

523
00:30:58,993 --> 00:30:59,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

524
00:30:59,293 --> 00:31:01,774
[SPEAKER_00]: Which makes possible, which makes it possible for a mood to change.

525
00:31:01,834 --> 00:31:02,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

526
00:31:03,155 --> 00:31:07,657
[SPEAKER_00]: If the current mood is the utmost, highest point of your mood, it could never change.

527
00:31:08,117 --> 00:31:08,858
[SPEAKER_00]: It would remain that.

528
00:31:11,002 --> 00:31:13,763
[SPEAKER_00]: So, good mood, bad mood, neutral mood, what's in common to that?

529
00:31:14,323 --> 00:31:15,144
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a mood.

530
00:31:15,424 --> 00:31:16,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

531
00:31:16,224 --> 00:31:22,027
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seated posture, standing, walking down, laying down, what's in common to it?

532
00:31:22,127 --> 00:31:22,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Body.

533
00:31:22,487 --> 00:31:23,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Body having to be there.

534
00:31:23,447 --> 00:31:29,530
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you get to discern the identity of the phenomenon of the enduring body

535
00:31:30,240 --> 00:31:33,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Through being mindful of a particular context of your posture.

536
00:31:33,421 --> 00:31:35,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Not through, like, I'm seated, I'm seated, I'm seated.

537
00:31:35,842 --> 00:31:37,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Nāma knees, nāma ankles.

538
00:31:37,203 --> 00:31:38,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Nāma knees, nāma ankles, nāma feet.

539
00:31:39,124 --> 00:31:44,786
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't need to do that, because you already know where you are and what you are, and the designation of seated body already is there.

540
00:31:44,826 --> 00:31:46,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I ask you, are you seated now?

541
00:31:47,547 --> 00:31:49,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you need to stop and think about it?

542
00:31:49,308 --> 00:31:52,689
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, start body sweeping from your toes to the top of your head?

543
00:31:52,850 --> 00:31:52,990
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

544
00:31:53,230 --> 00:31:54,390
[SPEAKER_00]: You would know, of course, I'm seated.

545
00:31:54,710 --> 00:31:55,791
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just thinking...

546
00:31:55,811 --> 00:31:55,911
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

547
00:31:59,484 --> 00:32:01,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, just playing with this image.

548
00:32:02,225 --> 00:32:02,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Put it down.

549
00:32:04,586 --> 00:32:12,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Playing with this image I have of what my body looks like spatially, based on the fact that I know I'm sitting down.

550
00:32:13,069 --> 00:32:13,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

551
00:32:13,610 --> 00:32:14,950
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't be possible if I didn't know.

552
00:32:15,010 --> 00:32:22,913
[SPEAKER_00]: And practically, that results in you putting the image of where you think the body is first, and not what your actual body is, which is the basis for that image.

553
00:32:23,053 --> 00:32:23,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

554
00:32:23,514 --> 00:32:23,714
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

555
00:32:24,414 --> 00:32:26,275
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you cannot be properly mindful.

556
00:32:26,435 --> 00:32:26,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

557
00:32:27,632 --> 00:32:28,533
[SPEAKER_00]: And you cannot sustain that.

558
00:32:28,573 --> 00:32:35,036
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, the moment you forget about that image, you have forgot the only reference point you knew, which was not even a reference point.

559
00:32:41,100 --> 00:32:50,025
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like trying to... trying to anchor your boat by throwing, like, the floating... those floating cones.

560
00:32:51,425 --> 00:32:52,926
[SPEAKER_00]: They just stay on the surface of the water.

561
00:32:53,547 --> 00:32:54,747
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you float with it.

562
00:32:55,087 --> 00:32:56,108
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not anchored.

563
00:32:56,988 --> 00:32:59,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not discerning through what's there.

564
00:33:00,809 --> 00:33:03,409
[SPEAKER_00]: So, at the surface, I see what I attend to.

565
00:33:04,209 --> 00:33:05,470
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think that's what mindfulness is.

566
00:33:05,590 --> 00:33:07,390
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all I do is just keep clutching the surface.

567
00:33:07,730 --> 00:33:09,810
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, just not seeing the necessary basis.

568
00:33:09,890 --> 00:33:10,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

569
00:33:11,090 --> 00:33:14,791
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason you can attend to different parts of your body is because the body is already there.

570
00:33:14,891 --> 00:33:15,691
[SPEAKER_00]: And you already know it.

571
00:33:16,651 --> 00:33:19,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when you're attending to your knees, you're not attending to your head.

572
00:33:19,772 --> 00:33:23,653
[SPEAKER_00]: But attending to your head is a possibility in the background of your attention.

573
00:33:24,093 --> 00:33:25,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why you can then go and

574
00:33:25,749 --> 00:33:27,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Turn to it and attend to the head.

575
00:33:28,071 --> 00:33:32,694
[SPEAKER_00]: The space already, the nature of space is already a nature of possibilities in different directions.

576
00:33:33,375 --> 00:33:35,517
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already within the body being there.

577
00:33:35,937 --> 00:33:41,081
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the entire universe is contained within this body less than two meters tall, as the Buddha said it.

578
00:33:44,644 --> 00:33:45,424
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the whole point.

579
00:33:45,444 --> 00:33:48,607
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like when you sit to practice your meditation or your mindfulness.

580
00:33:48,647 --> 00:33:50,788
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about I must stay mindful with every second.

581
00:33:50,808 --> 00:33:50,928
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

582
00:33:50,968 --> 00:33:52,590
[SPEAKER_00]: I must learn how to forget mindfully.

583
00:33:53,711 --> 00:33:55,332
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to allow yourself to forget.

584
00:33:56,330 --> 00:34:01,914
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when you un-forget it, you don't induce confusion about it.

585
00:34:01,974 --> 00:34:03,335
[SPEAKER_00]: If you do, that's will work.

586
00:34:03,496 --> 00:34:05,037
[SPEAKER_00]: That confusion needs to be clarified.

587
00:34:05,097 --> 00:34:05,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but...

588
00:34:05,857 --> 00:34:08,800
[SPEAKER_00]: I might... Like, see, even if you're confused, you don't need to try to get rid of it.

589
00:34:08,900 --> 00:34:09,540
[SPEAKER_00]: You include it.

590
00:34:09,600 --> 00:34:09,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

591
00:34:10,221 --> 00:34:12,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Within the body needing to be there.

592
00:34:12,102 --> 00:34:16,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, your doubt and any of the five hindrances are then seen in the right light.

593
00:34:16,185 --> 00:34:18,287
[SPEAKER_00]: They're secondary to the necessary base of the body.

594
00:34:18,327 --> 00:34:19,188
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't give...

595
00:34:19,268 --> 00:34:20,689
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't have the power over it.

596
00:34:21,850 --> 00:34:24,512
[SPEAKER_00]: But putting yourself first means putting the hindrances first.

597
00:34:25,990 --> 00:34:27,451
[SPEAKER_02]: They become obstacles.

598
00:34:27,491 --> 00:34:28,171
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where they hinder.

599
00:34:28,292 --> 00:34:28,652
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

600
00:34:28,832 --> 00:34:29,793
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

601
00:34:31,954 --> 00:34:36,657
[SPEAKER_01]: It seems to me that we... Just getting this include... including.

602
00:34:37,057 --> 00:34:37,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Learning how to include.

603
00:34:37,858 --> 00:34:38,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Learning how to include.

604
00:34:38,838 --> 00:34:39,499
[SPEAKER_00]: Including what?

605
00:34:39,679 --> 00:34:41,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Including the context that remains unchanged.

606
00:34:42,021 --> 00:34:46,163
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the necessary basis for the practice of including is discernment of the context.

607
00:34:46,604 --> 00:34:46,844
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

608
00:34:47,364 --> 00:34:51,086
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, as I was saying, that maybe... maybe now talk the other day.

609
00:34:51,827 --> 00:34:53,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Or maybe... Oh, somebody else.

610
00:34:54,065 --> 00:35:04,050
[SPEAKER_00]: When, you know, like you hear this generic instruction, just come back to the present moment, just let go, just let things go through, and it works for a day.

611
00:35:04,570 --> 00:35:05,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, okay.

612
00:35:05,491 --> 00:35:10,293
[SPEAKER_00]: It works initially, because the problem of your suffering is you resisting things.

613
00:35:10,513 --> 00:35:12,794
[SPEAKER_00]: And now there's this novelty that tells you don't resist.

614
00:35:13,635 --> 00:35:17,977
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't resist it, but then your context of it is still a context of resistance.

615
00:35:18,257 --> 00:35:20,258
[SPEAKER_00]: So then you start resisting that.

616
00:35:21,127 --> 00:35:30,750
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you come back to the present moment, works initially, helps you with whatever suffering, but then, oh, now you start doing that on account of trying to avoid suffering.

617
00:35:30,770 --> 00:35:31,810
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not including it.

618
00:35:32,751 --> 00:35:33,531
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the danger.

619
00:35:33,551 --> 00:35:35,592
[SPEAKER_00]: When I say you must include it, people will include it.

620
00:35:35,732 --> 00:35:36,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

621
00:35:36,272 --> 00:35:37,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you want to include it?

622
00:35:37,392 --> 00:35:45,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you want to get rid of that which bothers my point of view, which means my point of view is first and needs dealing with these particular things.

623
00:35:46,220 --> 00:35:50,903
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start doing mindfulness correctly for the wrong reasons, which means it's not the right mindfulness.

624
00:35:52,224 --> 00:35:55,346
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to see that your point of view is second, you have no say in it.

625
00:35:57,187 --> 00:36:05,853
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you wonder, oh my God, that will overwhelm me with anxiety and stuff, it might, which is why you need to develop sensory strength, precepts, unblemished virtue beforehand.

626
00:36:06,893 --> 00:36:08,434
[SPEAKER_00]: A layman or a monk, doesn't matter.

627
00:36:09,175 --> 00:36:10,756
[SPEAKER_01]: And pretty much, when you say that,

628
00:36:12,677 --> 00:36:15,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of anxiety is possibly a good sign.

629
00:36:16,641 --> 00:36:16,921
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

630
00:36:17,081 --> 00:36:18,002
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a good symptom, yeah.

631
00:36:18,723 --> 00:36:21,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you are anxious only because you don't know what to do.

632
00:36:22,927 --> 00:36:24,288
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize, no, that's the whole point.

633
00:36:24,328 --> 00:36:26,030
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about knowing what I must do.

634
00:36:26,531 --> 00:36:28,292
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about knowing what I will not do.

635
00:36:28,633 --> 00:36:28,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

636
00:36:29,730 --> 00:36:30,830
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you cannot be anxious.

637
00:36:30,850 --> 00:36:32,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Of being okay with not knowing.

638
00:36:32,691 --> 00:36:33,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

639
00:36:33,231 --> 00:36:34,852
[SPEAKER_00]: You become okay, which means you're not anxious.

640
00:36:35,172 --> 00:36:35,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

641
00:36:35,392 --> 00:36:36,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you realize, no, I do...

642
00:36:36,433 --> 00:36:37,653
[SPEAKER_00]: I know exactly what I want to do.

643
00:36:37,993 --> 00:36:38,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

644
00:36:38,313 --> 00:36:39,514
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all I need to worry about.

645
00:36:39,794 --> 00:36:42,315
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to worry about how will I figure this out, because I don't need to figure it out.

646
00:36:42,455 --> 00:36:42,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

647
00:36:43,155 --> 00:36:46,657
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason why I needed to figure it out is because I didn't know what I'm not supposed to do.

648
00:36:46,857 --> 00:36:47,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

649
00:36:57,176 --> 00:36:58,296
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, your mind drifts off.

650
00:36:58,396 --> 00:37:01,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you really remember that your body is seated here.

651
00:37:01,797 --> 00:37:02,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

652
00:37:02,077 --> 00:37:02,778
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same body.

653
00:37:02,798 --> 00:37:03,638
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same position.

654
00:37:04,338 --> 00:37:05,798
[SPEAKER_00]: And the drifting, there was a way...

655
00:37:05,818 --> 00:37:08,739
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to use that as a... yeah, what if, what if, what if... No.

656
00:37:08,919 --> 00:37:13,381
[SPEAKER_00]: That was within the body remaining unchanged all this time, even when I wasn't thinking about it.

657
00:37:14,721 --> 00:37:15,741
[SPEAKER_01]: But I wasn't practicing that.

658
00:37:16,081 --> 00:37:16,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

659
00:37:16,842 --> 00:37:20,963
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have this sense of duty, sense of my... this is what I must do.

660
00:37:22,130 --> 00:37:24,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Even that sense now, you don't need to act out of it.

661
00:37:24,472 --> 00:37:29,174
[SPEAKER_00]: You include it in the body being unchanged, remaining in the same seated position when you sat down to meditate.

662
00:37:30,435 --> 00:37:34,858
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you stop acting out of doubt, out of restlessness, out of worry, out of remorse.

663
00:37:36,179 --> 00:37:38,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you get to discern the context that's beyond it.

664
00:37:39,481 --> 00:37:40,702
[SPEAKER_00]: The necessary basis for it.

665
00:37:43,423 --> 00:37:48,667
[SPEAKER_00]: And saying, oh, I had a sensual thought, I had a sensual thought, within the context of the body that hasn't been acting sensually.

666
00:37:49,792 --> 00:37:50,352
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha says.

667
00:37:50,813 --> 00:37:54,875
[SPEAKER_00]: He would get to understand these things because his behavior has been purified beforehand.

668
00:37:55,475 --> 00:37:58,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the body has been factually not engaging with these things anymore.

669
00:37:58,837 --> 00:38:01,478
[SPEAKER_00]: That becomes your new container, your new reference point.

670
00:38:02,559 --> 00:38:05,201
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you get to approach it on the level of the mind eventually.

671
00:38:11,524 --> 00:38:16,407
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yeah, as the Buddha says, knowing thoughts as thoughts, feelings as feelings, the body as the body,

672
00:38:18,253 --> 00:38:27,716
[SPEAKER_03]: You're knowing... like, I've seen people, they meditate, and then they're like, oh, there was no more body, there were no more thoughts, I went beyond the mind, beyond the body.

673
00:38:29,037 --> 00:38:32,578
[SPEAKER_00]: It's true, in a sense, that they went beyond what they think the body is.

674
00:38:33,378 --> 00:38:35,619
[SPEAKER_00]: But that was a completely very, very limited perspective.

675
00:38:36,499 --> 00:38:42,181
[SPEAKER_00]: If you would get to understand what the body is, you realize, well, it's impossible for it to not be there as a necessary basis.

676
00:38:42,201 --> 00:38:45,742
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like saying, all the matter has disappeared, half of my aggregates were gone.

677
00:38:46,483 --> 00:38:47,303
[SPEAKER_00]: That's inconceivable.

678
00:38:48,652 --> 00:38:51,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're just removing all the wrong views and assumptions you would have.

679
00:38:51,675 --> 00:38:52,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

680
00:38:52,596 --> 00:38:53,097
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the thing.

681
00:38:53,377 --> 00:38:54,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Body in the body.

682
00:38:54,038 --> 00:38:56,140
[SPEAKER_00]: People think I must attend to the body within the body.

683
00:38:56,160 --> 00:38:56,220
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

684
00:38:56,240 --> 00:38:58,302
[SPEAKER_00]: It means seeing the body within the body.

685
00:38:58,322 --> 00:39:03,188
[SPEAKER_00]: It means seeing the body within whatever particular is happening with the body, you still... the nature of the body is there.

686
00:39:03,368 --> 00:39:04,469
[SPEAKER_00]: So, body within the body.

687
00:39:05,310 --> 00:39:06,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, my body is seated.

688
00:39:06,191 --> 00:39:07,172
[SPEAKER_00]: My itching moving.

689
00:39:10,668 --> 00:39:14,510
[SPEAKER_00]: All these things of the body that you can attend are possible because the body is there.

690
00:39:15,131 --> 00:39:19,773
[SPEAKER_00]: All these things within the feeling that you can attend on account of your feeling are because feeling is there.

691
00:39:20,414 --> 00:39:21,635
[SPEAKER_00]: So, feeling within the feeling.

692
00:39:23,135 --> 00:39:31,881
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, discerning the nature and not being sort of gripped and losing the sight on account of the particular thing that pulled your attention.

693
00:39:31,901 --> 00:39:35,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Like remaining impenetrable with regard to discerning the nature through that.

694
00:39:36,123 --> 00:39:38,805
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you say it's not just seeing the nature, but seeing...

695
00:39:40,982 --> 00:39:50,829
[SPEAKER_01]: That any particular manifestation of this thing is within, is enclosed within, is subordinate to the nature of that thing.

696
00:39:50,989 --> 00:39:51,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

697
00:39:51,589 --> 00:39:53,691
[SPEAKER_00]: The relationship between the nature and the particular.

698
00:39:54,091 --> 00:39:56,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you're just looking for the nature, it will become the new particular.

699
00:39:56,953 --> 00:39:58,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

700
00:39:58,894 --> 00:40:00,195
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the entire bodhisattva upādāna.

701
00:40:00,335 --> 00:40:01,216
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that relationship.

702
00:40:01,356 --> 00:40:01,876
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, yeah.

703
00:40:01,896 --> 00:40:03,717
[SPEAKER_00]: With this, this is.

704
00:40:03,737 --> 00:40:07,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't go into with what, what is.

705
00:40:07,440 --> 00:40:08,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Just with this, this is.

706
00:40:08,721 --> 00:40:09,661
[SPEAKER_00]: These two things are present.

707
00:40:09,681 --> 00:40:09,982
[SPEAKER_00]: With this,

708
00:40:11,042 --> 00:40:13,285
[SPEAKER_00]: This has to be.

709
00:40:13,325 --> 00:40:17,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you say, say, with the particular, there is the nature, there is the general, there is the... Yeah, of course.

710
00:40:17,991 --> 00:40:18,372
[SPEAKER_01]: ...essence.

711
00:40:18,392 --> 00:40:20,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Particular, as you say, is the first thing.

712
00:40:20,294 --> 00:40:20,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

713
00:40:20,795 --> 00:40:21,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Gets recognized.

714
00:40:21,836 --> 00:40:26,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Through which you discern the essence, because of which you realize that's why particular is there.

715
00:40:26,662 --> 00:40:28,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the two sticks propping each other.

716
00:40:28,384 --> 00:40:28,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

717
00:40:29,105 --> 00:40:29,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

718
00:40:32,413 --> 00:40:38,734
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why there is no ultimate saṅkhara you need to discern, or the ultimate nature, the true nature that underlines the entire universe.

719
00:40:38,794 --> 00:40:45,676
[SPEAKER_00]: It's right here, in this body, less than two meters tall, in your actions, in your speech, in your thoughts, that principle needs to be there.

720
00:40:45,796 --> 00:40:48,636
[SPEAKER_00]: And seeing that will uproot the passion from all these domains.

721
00:40:49,296 --> 00:40:50,597
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all you need to do.

722
00:40:53,137 --> 00:40:53,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

723
00:40:53,397 --> 00:40:56,998
[SPEAKER_00]: Because seeing that it's these two things that are mutually determining each other,

724
00:40:57,637 --> 00:41:01,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Leaves no room for the sense of self, the owner, that's first controlling them all.

725
00:41:01,559 --> 00:41:01,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

726
00:41:01,839 --> 00:41:02,820
[SPEAKER_00]: That depends on that.

727
00:41:02,840 --> 00:41:03,660
[SPEAKER_00]: That depends on that.

728
00:41:03,961 --> 00:41:04,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

729
00:41:04,301 --> 00:41:06,142
[SPEAKER_00]: If I look at this, I see this behind it.

730
00:41:06,682 --> 00:41:08,203
[SPEAKER_00]: If I look at that, I see this behind it.

731
00:41:08,223 --> 00:41:08,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

732
00:41:08,403 --> 00:41:09,184
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same thing.

733
00:41:10,344 --> 00:41:11,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, where am I?

734
00:41:12,585 --> 00:41:15,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Wherever I am, I cannot be outside of this dependence.

735
00:41:15,367 --> 00:41:15,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

736
00:41:16,047 --> 00:41:17,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when you already start practicing anatta.

737
00:41:19,109 --> 00:41:21,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Not by denying yourself, but by including it in the right order.

738
00:41:22,151 --> 00:41:25,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Order that is dependable upon these things that are not in your control.

739
00:41:58,020 --> 00:42:04,044
[SPEAKER_01]: So, practice of mindfulness is practice of mindful recollected forgetfulness.

740
00:42:05,525 --> 00:42:06,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Learning how to forget.

741
00:42:06,305 --> 00:42:07,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Learning how to forget mindfully.

742
00:42:08,046 --> 00:42:18,093
[SPEAKER_02]: Without inducing... How to forget without... Yeah, learning how to forget without your forgetting inducing confusion and ignorance.

743
00:42:18,793 --> 00:42:25,117
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it's induced, you include it in the necessary basis that was necessary for your experience of forgetting.

744
00:42:25,157 --> 00:42:26,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Learning how to forget in the right order.

745
00:42:27,359 --> 00:42:27,539
Yeah.

746
00:42:29,474 --> 00:42:32,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Or not perverting the order on account of your forgetting.

747
00:42:32,817 --> 00:42:34,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's actually impossible to pervert the order.

748
00:42:35,018 --> 00:42:36,600
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter whether you forget about it or not.

749
00:42:36,900 --> 00:42:40,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Being clear about it, you'll stop being wound up by your forgetting.

750
00:42:40,843 --> 00:42:43,945
[SPEAKER_00]: And equally, you'll stop acting out of passion on account of your forgetting.

751
00:42:44,005 --> 00:42:45,126
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you won't be forgetting.

752
00:42:45,507 --> 00:42:48,789
[SPEAKER_00]: And forgetting, though, just says the same lecture of anything else.

753
00:42:49,590 --> 00:42:50,290
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an experience.

754
00:42:50,310 --> 00:42:50,791
[SPEAKER_01]: That's right.

755
00:42:50,811 --> 00:42:57,336
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I have the experience of forgetfulness on account of the present body enduring here, present feeling enduring here, which you cannot forget.

756
00:42:58,469 --> 00:42:59,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, forgotten means it's gone.

757
00:42:59,751 --> 00:43:00,491
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be gone.

758
00:43:03,334 --> 00:43:06,317
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's in the sphere of knowledge, as we discussed that before.

759
00:43:07,118 --> 00:43:11,142
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to go around thinking that two and two is four, two and two is four.

760
00:43:11,202 --> 00:43:12,563
[SPEAKER_00]: You know that always.

761
00:43:15,807 --> 00:43:16,868
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to think about it.

762
00:43:17,028 --> 00:43:21,572
[SPEAKER_00]: It's there, because it's part of the level of understanding now how two and two work.

763
00:43:24,083 --> 00:43:33,809
[SPEAKER_00]: So this, you understand the order, you're fully clear about the order, it's impossible to pervert the order through the confusion about the understanding, means mindfulness is effortless.

764
00:43:33,829 --> 00:43:34,510
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the thing, isn't it?

765
00:43:34,530 --> 00:43:35,310
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same thing.

766
00:43:35,530 --> 00:43:38,572
[SPEAKER_01]: With two and two plus four, you don't keep meddling with it.

767
00:43:38,592 --> 00:43:47,138
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have this nervous sense that I've got to keep going back to it, because you realize that two and two and four, no matter what I do, there's nothing I need to do.

768
00:43:47,918 --> 00:43:50,840
[SPEAKER_01]: So I'll just stop doing anything with regard to it, because I don't need, you know...

769
00:43:51,200 --> 00:43:51,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

770
00:43:52,241 --> 00:43:52,641
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

771
00:43:53,062 --> 00:43:57,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever you're experiencing, whatever you're meddling with, it's on account of the body being there.

772
00:43:57,545 --> 00:44:01,768
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on account of the feelings enduring there, unchanging their identity.

773
00:44:02,649 --> 00:44:06,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, they might be changing momentarily, while remaining the same feeling.

774
00:44:07,793 --> 00:44:10,475
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on account of the mind being there, on account of the thoughts being there.

775
00:44:20,142 --> 00:44:20,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

776
00:44:20,568 --> 00:44:21,421
[SPEAKER_00]: The boys are restless.

